The Java code solution I provided earlier would not be able to handle more complex negative sentences like "I am not at all interested in that offer". To handle such cases, we need to modify our approach. One way to do this is to use regular expressions to identify the negation words and their scope. We can then use a separate boolean variable to keep track of whether the current sentence is negated or not. Then, while iterating through each word of the sentence, we can check if the current word is a negation word and update the boolean variable accordingly. If the boolean variable is true, we append the opposite meaning of the word to our positive sentence. If it is false, we simply append the word as is. Once we have looped through all the words, we can remove any extra space at the end and add a period to complete the positive sentence. Here is an example Java code that implements this approach:

```java
import java.util.regex.Pattern;

public class NegativeToPositive {
 public static void main(String[] args) {
 String negativeSentence = "I am not at all interested in that offer.";
 String[] words = negativeSentence.split(" ");
 StringBuilder positiveSentence = new StringBuilder();
 boolean isNegated = false;
 Pattern negationPattern = Pattern.compile("b(no(t)?|never|none|nobody|nothing|nowhere|hardly|barely|scarcely|doesn't|isn't|wasn't|can't|won't|shouldn't|wouldn't|doesn't)?b",
 Pattern.CASE_INSENSITIVE);
 for (String word : words) {
 if (negationPattern.matcher(word).matches()) {
 isNegated = !isNegated;
 } else {
 positiveSentence.append(isNegated ? getOpposite(word) : word).append(" ");
 }
 }
 positiveSentence.deleteCharAt(positiveSentence.length() - 1);
 positiveSentence.append(".");
 System.out.println("Negative sentence: " + negativeSentence);
 System.out.println("Positive sentence: " + positiveSentence);
 }

 private static String getOpposite(String word) {
 // Return opposite meaning of the word
 switch (word.toLowerCase()) {
 case "interested":
 return "uninterested";
 case "happy":
 return "unhappy";
 case "good":
 return "bad";
 default:
 return "not " + word;
 }
 }
}
```
This updated solution uses a regular expression pattern to identify negation words and their variations. It also includes a helper method `getOpposite()` which returns the opposite meaning of certain words, like "interested" or "happy". This will allow the program to handle more complex negative sentences and still provide accurate positive sentences.

