```
import edu.stanford.nlp.simple.*;
public class PronounReplacer {
 public static void main(String[] args) {
 String sentence = "The cat chased the mouse.";
 Sentence parsedSentence = new Sentence(sentence);
 String[] tokens = parsedSentence.words().toArray(new String[0]);
 String[] tags = parsedSentence.posTags().toArray(new String[0]);
 for (int i = 0; i < tokens.length; i++) {
 if (tags[i].startsWith("N")) {
 String pronoun = PronounUtils.getPronoun(tokens[i], tags[i]);
 if (pronoun != null) {
 tokens[i] = pronoun;
 }
 }
 }
 String newSentence = String.join(" ", tokens);
 System.out.println(newSentence);
 }
}
class PronounUtils {
 public static String getPronoun(String noun, String tag) {
 if (tag.equals("NN")) {
 return "it";
 } else if (tag.equals("NNS")) {
 return "they";
 } else if (tag.equals("NNP")) {
 return "he";
 } else if (tag.equals("NNPS")) {
 return "they";
 } else {
 return null;
 }
 }
}
```
In this program, we use the Stanford CoreNLP library to parse the sentence and extract the parts of speech for each word. We then loop through each word and check if it is a noun (indicated by a part of speech tag starting with "N"). If it is a noun, we use a helper function `getPronoun()` to determine the appropriate pronoun to replace it with. Finally, we join the tokens back together to form the new sentence with pronouns.
When we run this program with the input sentence "The cat chased the mouse.", it will output "It chased them.".

