Here is the step-by-step code implementation in Java:
```
public class DivideAndConquer {
 public static void main(String[] args) {
 int[] arr = {5, 3, 1, 9, 4};
 int max = findMax(arr, 0, arr.length-1);
 int min = findMin(arr, 0, arr.length-1);
 System.out.println("Maximum value: " + max);
 System.out.println("Minimum value: " + min);
 }
 public static int findMax(int[] arr, int start, int end) {
 if (start == end) {
 return arr[start];
 } else if (end - start == 1) {
 return Math.max(arr[start], arr[end]);
 } else {
 int mid = (start + end) / 2;
 int max1 = findMax(arr, start, mid);
 int max2 = findMax(arr, mid+1, end);
 return Math.max(max1, max2);
 }
 }
 public static int findMin(int[] arr, int start, int end) {
 if (start == end) {
 return arr[start];
 } else if (end - start == 1) {
 return Math.min(arr[start], arr[end]);
 } else {
 int mid = (start + end) / 2;
 int min1 = findMin(arr, start, mid);
 int min2 = findMin(arr, mid+1, end);
 return Math.min(min1, min2);
 }
 }
}
```
Explanation:
The `findMax` function uses the divide and conquer algorithm to recursively find the maximum value in an array. It takes in three parameters: the array, the starting index, and the ending index. If the starting index and the ending index are the same, it means that there is only one element in the array, so we return that element. If there are two elements in the array, we compare them and return the maximum. If there are more than two elements, we divide the array into two halves and recursively call the `findMax` function on each half. We then compare the maximum values returned by each half and return the larger one.
The `findMin` function works similarly to the `findMax` function, but it finds the minimum value instead.
In the `main` function, we create an array of integers and call the `findMax` and `findMin` functions on it. We then print out the maximum and minimum values.

