One approach for finding the second largest integer element in an unsorted array is to iterate through the array and keep track of the largest and second largest elements seen so far. Here's a C++ function that implements this approach:
```
int findSecondLargest(int arr[], int n) {
 int largest = INT_MIN;
 int secondLargest = INT_MIN;
 for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
 if (arr[i] > largest) {
 secondLargest = largest;
 largest = arr[i];
 }
 else if (arr[i] > secondLargest && arr[i] != largest) {
 secondLargest = arr[i];
 }
 }
 if (secondLargest == INT_MIN) {
 cout << "There is no second largest element." << endl;
 return -1;
 }
 else {
 return secondLargest;
 }
}
```
This function takes an array of integers `arr` and its size `n` as inputs, and returns the second largest integer element in the array. The function first initializes the largest and second largest variables to the minimum integer value. It then iterates through the array, updating the largest and second largest variables as necessary. If there is no second largest element, the function returns -1 and displays a message indicating that there is no second largest element.
This algorithm has a time complexity of O(n), which is optimal for finding the second largest element in an unsorted array.

