To extract named entities from a sentence in PHP, you can use Natural Language Processing (NLP) libraries such as Stanford CoreNLP or OpenNLP. Here's an example using the Stanford CoreNLP library:
```php
// Load the Stanford CoreNLP library
require_once 'vendor/autoload.php';
$serializer = new SuperClosureSerializer();
// Set up the CoreNLP client
$client = new NlpToolsUtilsStanfordCoreNLPClient(
 'http://localhost:9000',
 $serializer
);
// Define the sentence to analyze
$sentence = 'He is going to the store at Berkeley tonight.';
// Send the sentence to the CoreNLP server for analysis
$annotations = $client->annotate($sentence);
// Extract named entities from the sentence
$namedEntities = array();
foreach ($annotations['sentences'][0]['tokens'] as $token) {
 if ($token['ner'] != 'O') {
 $namedEntities[] = $token['word'];
 }
}
// Output the named entity
if (count($namedEntities) > 0) {
 echo 'Named entity: ' . implode(' ', $namedEntities);
} else {
 echo 'No named entities found';
}
```
This code sends the sentence to a local instance of the Stanford CoreNLP server, which returns annotations including named entity recognition (NER) tags for each token in the sentence. The code then iterates through the tokens and extracts any tokens with a NER tag other than "O" (which stands for "other"). Finally, it outputs the named entity (if found) or a message indicating that no named entities were found.

